content.text:
**The package:** six files.
- Gemini's process description;
- YouTube's raw subtitles;
- the list of speech-to-text corrections;
- the audio-only transcript;
- the final transcript with the slides worked in;
- a summary.

An Opus worker is now checking it against the subtitles, the Keynote deck and the paper. Specifically, it checks:
- that each correction is right, especially on the technical terms;
- that nothing the speaker said was dropped or invented;
- that each slide sits where Park reaches it and its content is reproduced faithfully;
- that no slide or formula was made up.

It will fix the final transcript, or rebuild it from the audio-only one if that's better. Then it commits two files beside the paper's extract: the verified transcript and Gemini's process description. The subtitles, the intermediate files and the deck stay out of git.

**The proof: yes, I know what you mean.** The paper gives only proof sketches. It says the full proofs are in a companion "Extended Report" (Park et al. 2018), but cites it without a link.
- The KAIST lab's [publications page](https://plrg.kaist.ac.kr/research/publications) links it as "Extended Technical Report": https://drive.google.com/uc?export=download&id=19Fzopb-1bU-kYgX8ir9UGYdJO3BHvv7a
- From here, Google Drive asks for a sign-in, so I can't fetch it. Could you try that link in your browser and upload the PDF if it opens? Then it goes in beside the paper.
- Park's KAIST thesis on the same work may hold the proofs too, but the KAIST repository can't be reached from here.

**One thing for you to weigh before it's committed:** the paper is CC BY-NC, but I couldn't find a licence for the talk video or the deck. The transcript includes the slides' text. I'm committing it at your word, as with the paper, and will note the exception in the protocol's rule.

Sources:
- [KAIST PLRG publications](https://plrg.kaist.ac.kr/research/publications)
- [The talk on YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eqpe-VfW3F4)
- [POPL 2019 talk page](https://popl19.sigplan.org/details/POPL-2019-Research-Papers/76/Polymorphic-Symmetric-Multiple-Dispatch-with-Variance)
- [ACM DL entry](https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3290324)

Yes, it's the C4 handover note, and I answered on the page. Its first section has grown into an informal log: 27K of its 41K bytes are reports of single landings that the batch records, PLAN and FACTS already hold.

I've added your point to the goals: every kind of record gets one home with a stated purpose. The informal places are retired or folded into those homes:
- this section;
- the held list;
- PLAN's old rule sections;
- the boot note's standing lines.

The skill then says plainly what goes where.

You said the goals above it look right, so they're now recorded in POSITIONS, with this addition, as "The coordinator rewrite's goals".

The worker is running. It will:
- build the clean transcript from the two models, settling each disagreement by the slides and the paper;
- score Whisper and Qwen3-ASR on this talk;
- rewrite the talk file tight;
- keep the transcription scripts as a reusable tool, with the head-to-head results beside them;
- add what the talk adds to the paper to FACTS.

I'll bring you the comparison when it's done.

The sorting of the paper's and the talk's examples into tests waits for your go. I restated it, but you haven't said yes yet.

